#include <iostream>
#include <string>
int main() {
std::string str1 = "short";
// 'str1' is stored in a fixed-size buffer (usually 24-32 bytes)
std::string str2 = new std::string("much longer string");
// 'str2' uses dynamic memory allocation
}
```

:m Dynamic polymorphism in C++ refers to the ability of an object created from a derived class to replace an object created from a base class. It enables virtual functions that allow overridden methods in derived classes to be called through pointers or references to base classes. This provides flexibility and reusability by allowing different objects (derived types) to respond to the same method call in different ways.
```cpp
// Example of dynamic polymorphism using inheritance and virtual functions
class Shape {
public:
virtual void draw() = 0; // Pure virtual function
};
class Circle : public Shape {
public:
void draw() override { /* Draw a circle */ }
};
Shape* shapePtr;
shapePtr = new Circle(); // Polymorphic behavior
shapePtr->draw();
```

