:m Both `std::partition` and `std::sort` take a predicate function or comparator as an argument, which allows injecting specific behaviors at runtime. For example:
```cpp
namespace std {
template <typename ForwardIt, typename UnaryPredicate>
constexpr ForwardIt partition(ForwardIt first, ForwardIt last, UnaryPredicate p);
template <typename RandomIt, typename Compare>
constexpr void sort(RandomIt first, RandomIt last, Compare comp);
}
```
The `UnaryPredicate` for `std::partition` and the `Compare` for `std::sort` allow you to specify how elements should be ordered or partitioned without modifying the algorithms themselves.
This separation of concerns makes these functions more versatile and allows them to work with different data types and orderings.

:m The `std::unique_ptr` demonstrates policy-based design by using a template parameter to inject cleanup behavior. You can specify how memory should be deallocated when the pointer is destroyed.
Example:
```cpp
