:m The statement \( (P \rightarrow Q) \leftrightarrow (\neg P \vee Q) \) is a tautology because it expresses that an implication \(P \rightarrow Q\) is logically equivalent to the disjunction of the negation of \(P\) and \(Q\). This can be verified by constructing a truth table or by understanding that if \(P\) is false, then \(P \rightarrow Q\) is true regardless of \(Q\), which aligns with \(\neg P \vee Q\) being true. If \(P\) is true, then \(Q\) must also be true for the implication to hold, again matching \(\neg P \vee Q\).
