:m To prove that \(k(x) = x^2\) on \(\mathbb{R}^+\) is surjective, we need to show that every element in the codomain \(\mathbb{R}^+\) has at least one preimage in the domain. For any \(y \in \mathbb{R}^+\), there exists a unique positive real number \(x = \sqrt{y}\) such that:
\[ k(x) = x^2 = (\sqrt{y})^2 = y \]
Thus, for every element in the codomain \(\mathbb{R}^+\), there is at least one preimage in the domain, proving that \(k(x)\) is surjective.
