:m To prove that a function \( f \) is bijective, we first show it is an injection and then a surjection. Given \( f:A \rightarrow B \), assume \( f(a_1) = f(a_2) \). Since \( f^{-1} : B \rightarrow A \), applying \( f^{-1} \) to both sides gives:
\[ f(a_1) = f(a_2) \implies f^{-1}(f(a_1)) = f^{-1}(f(a_2)) \implies a_1 = a_2 \]
This proves that \( f \) is an injection. For surjection, for every \( b \in B \), there exists some \( a \in A \) such that \( f(a) = b \).
