:m In model-based reinforcement learning (RL), the agent constructs a model of the environment that can be used to predict future states and rewards given current actions. This allows for planning by considering potential sequences of events before they occur. In contrast, model-free RL directly interacts with the environment without explicitly constructing a model, focusing on learning through trial-and-error.
For example:
```java
// Model-based method pseudo-code
class ModelBasedAgent {
EnvironmentModel model;
void planNextAction() {
State initialState = getCurrentState();
Action bestAction = null;
double maxExpectedReward = -Infinity;
for (Action action : possibleActions) {
State nextState = model.predictNextState(initialState, action);
double reward = estimateFutureRewards(nextState);
if (reward > maxExpectedReward) {
bestAction = action;
maxExpectedReward = reward;
}
}
takeAction(bestAction);
}
}
```

:m In reinforcement learning, the state encapsulates the relevant information about the environment that the agent uses to decide its next action. The formal definition of a state follows the Markov decision process framework (MDP), but informally, it can be thought of as any information available to the agent about its surroundings.
